= 1
{parent=Paper 102}
{scope}

= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For a finite-dimensional complex <Lie algebra> $\mathfrak g$, the following three conditions are equivalent descriptions of a <semisimple Lie algebra>.

First, its <solvable radical> is zero. An <ideal of a Lie algebra> is a linear subspace $I$ satisfying $[\mathfrak g,I]\subseteq I$. A <solvable Lie algebra> is one whose <derived series>, defined by $I^{(0)}=I$ and $I^{(r+1)}=[I^{(r)},I^{(r)}]$, eventually becomes zero. The <solvable radical> is the largest solvable <ideal of a Lie algebra>; its vanishing is equivalent to the absence of nonzero solvable ideals.

Second, the <Killing form>
$$
\kappa(u,v)=\operatorname{tr}(\operatorname{ad}u\operatorname{ad}v)
$$
is a <nondegenerate bilinear form>: if $\kappa(u,v)=0$ for every $v$, then $u=0$. Here $\operatorname{ad}u(v)=[u,v]$ is the <adjoint representation>, and $\operatorname{tr}$ is the <trace>. This equivalence is the <Cartan criterion for semisimplicity>.

Third, $\mathfrak g$ is a <direct sum of Lie algebras> that are nonabelian <simple Lie algebras>. A <simple Lie algebra> is nonabelian and has no <ideals of a Lie algebra> other than zero and itself. Thus the three equivalent criteria are
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{rad}\mathfrak g=0\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad\kappa\text{ nondegenerate}\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad\mathfrak g=\bigoplus_j\mathfrak g_j\text{ with each }\mathfrak g_j\text{ simple}.}
$$

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <derived series> gives a direct test, with no structural theorem needed. By bilinearity and antisymmetry of the <Lie bracket>, every bracket in $\mathfrak h$ is a multiple of $z$, and $z=[x,y]$ actually occurs. Therefore
$$
\mathfrak h^{(1)}=[\mathfrak h,\mathfrak h]=\mathbb Cz,\qquad
\mathfrak h^{(2)}=[\mathbb Cz,\mathbb Cz]=0.
$$
Consequently this <Heisenberg Lie algebra> is a nonzero <solvable Lie algebra>. It is an <ideal of a Lie algebra> in itself, since $[\mathfrak h,\mathfrak h]\subseteq\mathfrak h$. Its <solvable radical> is therefore all of $\mathfrak h$, contradicting the first criterion in part (a). Hence
$$
\boxed{\mathfrak h\text{ is not semisimple}.}
$$

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

A two-dimensional <Lie algebra representation> already provides a counterexample. In the basis $e_1,e_2$ of $V=\mathbb C^2$, define
$$
\rho(x)=\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\0&0\end{pmatrix},\qquad
\rho(y)=\rho(z)=0.
$$
The commutator $[\rho(x),\rho(y)]$ is zero, agreeing with $\rho(z)$; the other defining <Lie brackets> also map to zero. Thus $\rho$ is a <Lie algebra homomorphism>, although it is not a <faithful Lie algebra representation>.

The line $L=\mathbb Ce_1$ is an <invariant subspace>. Any complementary line has the form $\mathbb C(e_2+a e_1)$ for some $a\in\mathbb C$. But
$$
\rho(x)(e_2+a e_1)=e_1\notin\mathbb C(e_2+a e_1),
$$
so no complementary line is an <invariant subspace>. Equivalently, every invariant line is killed by $\rho(x)$: its restriction to a line is a scalar, and its square is zero, forcing that scalar to be zero. A decomposition into two invariant lines would then force $\rho(x)=0$, a contradiction. This representation is therefore not a <semisimple representation>. Thus
$$
\boxed{\text{Not every finite-dimensional representation of }\mathfrak h\text{ is completely reducible}.}
$$

= 2
{parent=Paper 102}
{scope}

= a
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Use the <coroot> normalization $\alpha^\vee=2\alpha/(\alpha,\alpha)$, with the pairing understood via the <root system> and its invariant <inner product>. A <dominant integral weight>, called a <dominant weight> here, is a functional $\lambda\in\mathfrak t^*$ such that
$$
\boxed{\langle\lambda,\alpha_i^\vee\rangle\in\mathbb Z_{\geq0}\quad\text{for every simple root }\alpha_i.}
$$
Equivalently, $\lambda=\sum_i n_i\omega_i$ with $n_i\in\mathbb Z_{\geq0}$, where the <fundamental weights> satisfy $\langle\omega_i,\alpha_j^\vee\rangle=\delta_{ij}$. If dominance and integrality are defined separately, dominance means the inequalities and membership in the <weight lattice> means integrality.

A <highest-weight vector> is a nonzero <weight vector> $v\in V_\lambda$, so $h\cdot v=\lambda(h)v$ for all $h\in\mathfrak t$, annihilated by every positive <root space>:
$$
\boxed{v\ne0,\quad h\cdot v=\lambda(h)v,\quad\mathfrak g_\beta\cdot v=0\quad(\beta\in\Phi^+).}
$$
The last condition is equivalently annihilation by the <raising operators> for the <simple roots>.

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Fix a <simple root> $\alpha_i$ and choose generators $e_i,f_i,h_i$ of the <sl2 subalgebra associated with a root>, with $h_i=\alpha_i^\vee$ and
$$
[h_i,e_i]=2e_i,\qquad[h_i,f_i]=-2f_i,\qquad[e_i,f_i]=h_i.
$$
The <highest-weight vector> satisfies $e_i v=0$ and $h_i v=m_i v$, where $m_i=\langle\lambda,\alpha_i^\vee\rangle$. The <classification of finite-dimensional sl2 representations> already implies that $m_i$ is a nonnegative integer. One can also see the necessary integrality directly from the <lowering operator>.

Indeed, $f_i^k v$, if nonzero, has $h_i$ eigenvalue $m_i-2k$. These distinct <eigenvalues> cannot occur indefinitely in a finite-dimensional space, so there is a largest $r\geq0$ with $f_i^r v\ne0$. The <sl2 Lie algebra> relations imply, by induction on $k$,
$$
e_i f_i^k v=k(m_i-k+1)f_i^{k-1}v.
$$
For $k=r+1$ the left side is zero, hence $(r+1)(m_i-r)f_i^r v=0$. Thus $m_i=r\in\mathbb Z_{\geq0}$. Since this holds for every <simple root>,
$$
\boxed{\lambda\text{ is a dominant integral weight}.}
$$

= c
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Choose the given <short root> $\alpha$ as a <simple root>, and let $\beta$ be the long <simple root>. This entails no loss of generality: the <Weyl group> is transitive on the <short roots> of the <G2 root system>. The relevant <Cartan integers> are $\langle\beta,\alpha^\vee\rangle=-3$ and $\langle\alpha,\beta^\vee\rangle=-1$. The <positive roots> are
$$
\alpha,\ \beta,\ \alpha+\beta,\ 2\alpha+\beta,\ 3\alpha+\beta,\ 3\alpha+2\beta.
$$
Under $\mathfrak m_\alpha\cong\mathfrak{sl}_2$, the <root space> for $m\alpha+n\beta$ has $h_\alpha$ eigenvalue $2m-3n$. The subalgebra $\mathfrak m_\alpha$ itself is the three-dimensional irreducible $V(2)$ in the <adjoint representation>.

The four <root spaces> along the <root string> $\beta,\alpha+\beta,2\alpha+\beta,3\alpha+\beta$ have eigenvalues $-3,-1,1,3$. Consecutive spaces are connected by nonzero <raising operators> and <lowering operators>, so their sum is $V(3)$. The negative <root string> supplies another $V(3)$. The <root spaces> for $\pm(3\alpha+2\beta)$ have eigenvalue zero, and neither adding nor subtracting $\alpha$ gives a root; they are two copies of $V(0)$. Finally, the one-dimensional space $\ker\alpha\subset\mathfrak t$ commutes with $\mathfrak m_\alpha$, giving one more $V(0)$. We have accounted for all $14$ dimensions, and therefore the <G2 adjoint branching to a short-root sl2 subalgebra> is
$$
\boxed{\mathfrak g\cong V(3)^{\oplus2}\oplus V(2)\oplus V(0)^{\oplus3}.}
$$
For a nonzero <root vector> $x\in\mathfrak g_\alpha$, its <adjoint representation> action is a nonzero scalar multiple of the <raising operator> on each summand. On each irreducible $V(n)$ this operator has a one-dimensional <kernel>, including $V(0)$. Since the <Lie algebra centralizer> is that <kernel>,
$$
\boxed{\dim Z_{\mathfrak g}(x)=2+1+3=6.}
$$

= d
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Suppose first that $x\ne0$, and let $E$ be the real span of the <root system>, of dimension equal to the <rank of a semisimple Lie algebra> $\ell$. The images of the roots span $E/\mathbb R\alpha$, so select roots $\beta_1,\ldots,\beta_{\ell-1}$ whose images form a basis of this quotient.

For each $i$, take the highest endpoint $\gamma_i^+$ of the $\alpha$ <root string> through $\beta_i$, and the highest endpoint $\gamma_i^-$ of the <root string> through $-\beta_i$. By construction, $\gamma_i^\pm+\alpha$ is not a root. Moreover $\gamma_i^\pm\ne-\alpha$, since their images in $E/\mathbb R\alpha$ are nonzero. The <root-space decomposition> and its bracket rule therefore give
$$
[\mathfrak g_{\gamma_i^\pm},x]=0.
$$
These $2(\ell-1)$ roots are distinct: their quotient images are the two signs of a basis, which are distinct. Their one-dimensional <root spaces> consequently contribute $2(\ell-1)$ independent vectors to the <Lie algebra centralizer>.

In addition, the $\ell-1$ dimensional subspace $\ker\alpha\subset\mathfrak t$ commutes with $x$, because $[h,x]=\alpha(h)x$. The line $\mathbb Cx\subset\mathfrak g_\alpha$ also commutes with $x$. These contributions are independent by the <root-space decomposition>; in particular none of the selected endpoint roots is $\alpha$. Thus the <centralizer lower bound for a root vector> is
$$
\boxed{\dim Z_{\mathfrak g}(x)\geq2(\ell-1)+(\ell-1)+1=3\ell-2.}
$$
If $x=0$, then $Z_{\mathfrak g}(x)=\mathfrak g$. The <root system> contains the $2\ell$ distinct roots $\pm\alpha_i$, so $\dim\mathfrak g=\ell+|\Phi|\geq3\ell$, which also proves the desired inequality. The argument includes rank one, where the list of $\beta_i$ is empty.

= 3
{parent=Paper 102}
{scope}

= a
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Write an element of the diagonal <Cartan subalgebra> as
$$
H(t)=\operatorname{diag}(t_1,t_2,t_3,-t_1,-t_2,-t_3),\qquad
\varepsilon_i(H(t))=t_i.
$$
The <root system> of this <special orthogonal Lie algebra> is
$$
\boxed{\Phi=\{\pm\varepsilon_i\pm\varepsilon_j:1\leq i<j\leq3\}.}
$$
Thus the maps requested are $H(t)\mapsto\pm t_i\pm t_j$, with the two signs independent. This is the <D3 root system>, with twelve roots.

For a direct check, the defining matrix identity gives the block form $X=\begin{pmatrix}A&B\\C&-A^T\end{pmatrix}$ with $B^T=-B$ and $C^T=-C$. If $E_{ij}$ denotes a <matrix unit>, the <root space> for $\varepsilon_i-\varepsilon_j$ is spanned by $E_{ij}-E_{j+3,i+3}$; those for $\varepsilon_i+\varepsilon_j$ and $-\varepsilon_i-\varepsilon_j$ are spanned respectively by $E_{i,j+3}-E_{j,i+3}$ and $E_{i+3,j}-E_{j+3,i}$. Commuting these with $H(t)$ gives exactly the displayed maps.

= b
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Choose the <root basis>
$$
\boxed{\alpha_1=\varepsilon_1-\varepsilon_2,\qquad
\alpha_2=\varepsilon_2-\varepsilon_3,\qquad
\alpha_3=\varepsilon_2+\varepsilon_3.}
$$
In the usual invariant <inner product>, each <simple root> has squared length $2$, while $(\alpha_1,\alpha_2)=(\alpha_1,\alpha_3)=-1$ and $(\alpha_2,\alpha_3)=0$. The labeled <Dynkin diagram> is therefore the following chain, with a single bond between adjacent nodes:
$$
\underset{\alpha_2}{\circ}\;\text{---}\;\underset{\alpha_1}{\circ}\;\text{---}\;\underset{\alpha_3}{\circ}.
$$
It is type $D_3$, equivalently the <A3 root system>. To check the <root basis> explicitly, the corresponding <positive roots> are $\alpha_1,\alpha_2,\alpha_3,\alpha_1+\alpha_2,\alpha_1+\alpha_3,\alpha_1+\alpha_2+\alpha_3$; these and their negatives are all twelve roots.

= c
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Let $s_i=w_{\alpha_i}$. The <root reflection> formula is $s_i(\gamma)=\gamma-\langle\gamma,\alpha_i^\vee\rangle\alpha_i$. Applying the <Cartan integers> from part (b), the images of the chosen <simple roots> are
$$
\boxed{\begin{array}{c|ccc}
 &\alpha_1&\alpha_2&\alpha_3\\\hline
s_1&-\alpha_1&\alpha_1+\alpha_2&\alpha_1+\alpha_3\\
s_2&\alpha_1+\alpha_2&-\alpha_2&\alpha_3\\
s_3&\alpha_1+\alpha_3&\alpha_2&-\alpha_3
\end{array}.}
$$
In particular, the two end-node <Weyl reflections> fix the opposite end node.

= d
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

An <automorphism of a root system> outside the <Weyl group> is the sign change
$$
\boxed{\tau(\varepsilon_1)=\varepsilon_1,\qquad
\tau(\varepsilon_2)=\varepsilon_2,\qquad
\tau(\varepsilon_3)=-\varepsilon_3.}
$$
It preserves the set $\{\pm\varepsilon_i\pm\varepsilon_j\}$, fixes $\alpha_1$, and interchanges $\alpha_2$ and $\alpha_3$. It therefore realizes the nontrivial symmetry of the <Dynkin diagram> while preserving the chosen <root basis>.

To verify that $\tau\notin W$, note that $s_1$ swaps $\varepsilon_1,\varepsilon_2$, $s_2$ swaps $\varepsilon_2,\varepsilon_3$, and $s_3$ swaps $\varepsilon_2,\varepsilon_3$ while changing both their signs. Every product of these generators is a signed permutation with an even number of sign changes, whereas $\tau$ has one sign change. Thus it cannot be a <Weyl group> element. The parity here concerns sign changes, rather than the determinant of the permutation.

= e
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

The <Dynkin diagram> in part (b) is a three-node chain, hence $D_3=A_3$. By the classification of complex <semisimple Lie algebras> by their <root systems>, this gives the <isomorphism between so6 and sl4>:
$$
\boxed{\mathfrak{so}_6\cong\mathfrak{sl}_4.}
$$
There is also a concrete realization. On $U=\mathbb C^4$, choose a nonzero alternating four-form $\mathrm{vol}$. The six-dimensional <exterior square> $\Lambda^2U$ carries the <nondegenerate> <symmetric bilinear form> $B$ specified by
$$
B(u\wedge v,w\wedge z)=\mathrm{vol}(u,v,w,z).
$$
It is symmetric because exchanging two pairs entails four transpositions, and it is nondegenerate because each basis bivector pairs nontrivially with its complementary pair. The <special linear Lie algebra> acts by $A(u\wedge v)=Au\wedge v+u\wedge Av$. Its action on $\Lambda^4U$ is multiplication by $\operatorname{tr}A=0$, so it preserves $B$. We obtain a nonzero <Lie algebra homomorphism> $\mathfrak{sl}_4\to\mathfrak{so}(\Lambda^2U,B)$. Its <kernel> is an <ideal of a Lie algebra>, hence zero because $\mathfrak{sl}_4$ is a <simple Lie algebra>. Both sides have dimension $15$, so this homomorphism is a <Lie algebra isomorphism>.

= 4
{parent=Paper 102}
{scope}

= a
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For a <dominant integral weight> $\lambda$, let $V(\lambda)$ be the finite-dimensional irreducible <highest-weight representation> with <highest weight> $\lambda$. Set $\rho=\frac12\sum_{\alpha\in\Phi^+}\alpha$, the <half-sum of positive roots>. With $\alpha^\vee$ the <coroot> of $\alpha$, the <Weyl dimension formula> is
$$
\boxed{\dim V(\lambda)=\prod_{\alpha\in\Phi^+}
\frac{\langle\lambda+\rho,\alpha^\vee\rangle}
{\langle\rho,\alpha^\vee\rangle}.}
$$
Here $\Phi^+$ is the <positive system of a root system> specified by the chosen <root basis>. The <fundamental weights> are the basis dual to the simple <coroots>: $\langle\omega_i,\alpha_j^\vee\rangle=\delta_{ij}$. Thus $\lambda=\sum_i a_i\omega_i$ with nonnegative integers $a_i$. The denominators are positive; equivalently one can use $(\lambda+\rho,\alpha)/(\rho,\alpha)$ in each factor, since the coroot normalization cancels.

= b
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Use the <C2 root system> realization in an orthonormal basis $\varepsilon_1,\varepsilon_2$ with <simple roots> $\alpha_1=\varepsilon_1-\varepsilon_2$ and $\alpha_2=2\varepsilon_2$. This numbers the <short root> first, as required. The <fundamental weights> and <half-sum of positive roots> are
$$
\omega_1=\varepsilon_1,\qquad\omega_2=\varepsilon_1+\varepsilon_2,\qquad
\rho=2\varepsilon_1+\varepsilon_2,
$$
so $\lambda+\rho=(a+b+2)\varepsilon_1+(b+1)\varepsilon_2$. For the four <positive roots>, the factors of the <Weyl dimension formula> are
$$
\begin{array}{c|c|c}
\alpha&\alpha^\vee&
\langle\lambda+\rho,\alpha^\vee\rangle/\langle\rho,\alpha^\vee\rangle\\\hline
\varepsilon_1-\varepsilon_2&\varepsilon_1-\varepsilon_2&a+1\\
2\varepsilon_2&\varepsilon_2&b+1\\
\varepsilon_1+\varepsilon_2&\varepsilon_1+\varepsilon_2&(a+2b+3)/3\\
2\varepsilon_1&\varepsilon_1&(a+b+2)/2
\end{array}.
$$
Multiplying yields the <Weyl dimension formula for C2>:
$$
\boxed{\dim V(a\omega_1+b\omega_2)=\frac{(a+1)(b+1)(a+b+2)(a+2b+3)}6.}
$$
As checks, $V(\omega_1)$ has dimension $4$, $V(\omega_2)$ dimension $5$, and $V(2\omega_1)$ dimension $10$.

= c
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

In the defining representation of the <symplectic Lie algebra> $\mathfrak{sp}_4$, the weights are $\varepsilon_1,\varepsilon_2,-\varepsilon_1,-\varepsilon_2$. Their <highest weight>, for the <root basis> of part (b), is therefore
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{highest\ weight}(V)=\varepsilon_1=\omega_1.}
$$
The flip on the <tensor square> commutes with the action of $\mathfrak{sp}_4$, giving $V\otimes V=\operatorname{Sym}^2V\oplus\Lambda^2V$ with dimensions $10$ and $6$.

If $v_1$ is a <highest-weight vector> of weight $\varepsilon_1$, then $v_1\otimes v_1$ is a <highest-weight vector> of weight $2\omega_1$ in the <symmetric square>. By <Weyl complete reducibility theorem>, this ensures an irreducible summand $V(2\omega_1)$ occurs. Its dimension is $10$ by part (b), exhausting the <symmetric square>.

Let $\omega$ denote the preserved <symplectic form>. The <symplectic contraction of an exterior square> is the nonzero equivariant map
$$
c:\Lambda^2V\longrightarrow\mathbb C,\qquad c(v\wedge w)=\omega(v,w),
$$
where the target is a <trivial Lie algebra representation>. Thus its <kernel> has dimension $5$. Choose a weight vector $v_2$ of weight $\varepsilon_2$ in a <symplectic basis>, with $\omega(v_1,v_2)=0$. The vector $v_1\wedge v_2$ belongs to this <kernel> and has weight $\varepsilon_1+\varepsilon_2=\omega_2$. It is a <highest-weight vector>: none of $\omega_2+\alpha$, for $\alpha\in\Phi^+$, is a weight of $\Lambda^2V$, whose weights are $\pm\varepsilon_1\pm\varepsilon_2$ and zero. Hence the <kernel> contains $V(\omega_2)$; its dimension $5$ exhausts the <kernel>. <Weyl complete reducibility theorem> supplies a complementary invariant line $V(0)$.

The resulting <tensor-square decomposition of the defining sp4 representation> is
$$
\boxed{V\otimes V\cong V(2\omega_1)\oplus V(\omega_2)\oplus V(0),\qquad16=10+5+1.}
$$

= 5
{parent=Paper 102}
{scope}

= a
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <formal character of a weight module> is $\operatorname{ch}V=\sum_\mu(\dim V_\mu)e^\mu$, where $e^\mu$ are formal basis symbols in the <group algebra> of the <weight lattice>, satisfying $e^\mu e^\nu=e^{\mu+\nu}$. For a finite-dimensional irreducible <highest-weight representation>, the <highest weight> has <weight multiplicity> one. Another standard result is that <weight multiplicities> are invariant under the <Weyl group>: $\dim V_{w\mu}=\dim V_\mu$.

A <minuscule representation> has all its weights in a single <Weyl group> orbit. Since the <highest weight> $\lambda$ is present, that orbit is $W\lambda$, and the invariance just stated shows every point of it is present with multiplicity one. Consequently the <formal character of a minuscule representation> is
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{ch}V(\lambda)=\sum_{\mu\in W\lambda}e^\mu,\qquad\dim V(\lambda)=|W\lambda|.}
$$
The sum is over distinct weights, rather than over all elements of $W$: summing $e^{w\lambda}$ over $w\in W$ would count each weight $|\operatorname{Stab}_W(\lambda)|$ times.

= b
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Use the <coroot> $\alpha^\vee$ in the pairing. The original PDF retains this mark, which is lost in the local TeX transcription. Fix any <positive root> $\alpha$, and put $n=\langle\lambda,\alpha^\vee\rangle$. For a <dominant integral weight>, $n$ is a nonnegative integer; positive <coroots> are nonnegative integral combinations of simple <coroots>.

Let $v_\lambda$ be a <highest-weight vector>, and restrict to the <sl2 subalgebra associated with a root> $\alpha$. It is killed by its <raising operator> $e_\alpha$ and has $h_\alpha$ eigenvalue $n$. If $n\geq1$, its <lowering operator> produces a nonzero vector: otherwise $[e_\alpha,f_\alpha]v_\lambda=n v_\lambda$ would vanish. Therefore $\lambda-\alpha$ is a weight.

Choose a <Weyl group> invariant positive-definite <inner product> on the real weight space. All weights of a <minuscule representation> have the same norm, since they belong to $W\lambda$. But
$$
\|\lambda-\alpha\|^2-\|\lambda\|^2
=-2(\lambda,\alpha)+(\alpha,\alpha)
=(1-n)(\alpha,\alpha).
$$
If $n\geq2$, this is negative, a contradiction. Thus for every <positive root>,
$$
\boxed{\langle\lambda,\alpha^\vee\rangle\in\{0,1\},\quad\text{in particular }\langle\lambda,\alpha^\vee\rangle\leq1.}
$$
When $n=1$, the lower weight is precisely the <Weyl reflection> $s_\alpha\lambda=\lambda-\alpha$, consistent with the orbit condition.

= c
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

We first prove the useful lemma that <dominant root-lattice highest weights have zero weight>. Write $Q=\mathbb Z\Phi$ for the <root lattice>, and let a <dominant integral weight> $\lambda\in Q$ be the <highest weight> of a finite-dimensional irreducible representation.

First, $\lambda$ is a nonnegative integral combination of <simple roots>. Indeed, write $\lambda=\lambda_+-\lambda_-$, separating its positive and negative coefficients in the <root basis>. The two parts have disjoint supports, and distinct <simple roots> have nonpositive <inner product>, so $(\lambda_+,\lambda_-)\leq0$. If $\lambda_-\ne0$, then
$$
(\lambda,\lambda_-)=(\lambda_+,\lambda_-)-(\lambda_-,\lambda_-)<0.
$$
On the other hand, dominance gives $(\lambda,\alpha_i)\geq0$ for each <simple root>, hence $(\lambda,\lambda_-)\geq0$. This contradiction proves $\lambda_-=0$.

Now suppose a nonzero weight $\mu=\sum_i m_i\alpha_i$ has all $m_i\in\mathbb Z_{\geq0}$. The identity
$$
0<(\mu,\mu)=\sum_i m_i(\mu,\alpha_i)
$$
shows that some $i$ satisfies $m_i>0$ and $\langle\mu,\alpha_i^\vee\rangle>0$. We use the standard <sl2 Lie algebra> fact that its <lowering operator> is injective on any positive $h$ eigenspace in a finite-dimensional representation. This follows from the <classification of finite-dimensional sl2 representations>: in each irreducible $V(n)$, the only weight killed by the <lowering operator> is the lowest weight $-n\leq0$.

Consequently a nonzero vector of weight $\mu$ lowers to a nonzero vector of weight $\mu-\alpha_i$. Its simple-root coefficients remain nonnegative and their sum decreases by one. Starting at $\lambda$, repeated lowering must therefore reach the zero weight. Notice that intermediate weights need not remain dominant; positivity of the chosen coroot pairing is enough at each step.

For a <minuscule representation>, every weight belongs to $W\lambda$, so the zero weight just obtained lies in this orbit. Every <Weyl group> element is invertible, and $w\lambda=0$ forces $\lambda=0$. This proves that <minuscule weights in the root lattice are zero>. Under the assumption $X=Q$, every possible <highest weight> lies in $Q$, hence
$$
\boxed{X=\mathbb Z\Phi\ \Longrightarrow\ \text{the only minuscule irreducible representation is }V(0),\text{ the trivial one}.}
$$
Here $V(0)$ is the one-dimensional <trivial Lie algebra representation>, by the classification of finite-dimensional irreducible <highest-weight representations>.
